WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.300
The first film showed how moving calcium creates a cytosolic rise.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:08.060
How can that same ion trigger different responses?

3
00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:14.380
A cell cannot label one calcium ion for contraction and another for secretion.

4
00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:19.600
Instead, it controls where the rise happens, and when and how often.

5
00:00:22.617 --> 00:00:24.197
Open one gold channel.

6
00:00:25.637 --> 00:00:30.237
Calcium is highest near its pore before buffers capture and pumps remove it.

7
00:00:31.297 --> 00:00:32.977
The teal plume fades with distance.

8
00:00:34.297 --> 00:00:37.997
A sensor anchored beside the channel samples that local domain.

9
00:00:39.317 --> 00:00:43.937
An identical sensor across the cytosol meets much less free calcium.

10
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.280
Predict before either sensor changes.

11
00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:52.160
Both sensors switch on only above the same calcium threshold.

12
00:00:53.500 --> 00:00:54.920
The channel opens briefly.

13
00:00:56.220 --> 00:01:00.660
Which sensor responds: the one beside the pore, the one across the cell, or both?

14
00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:03.220
Follow the fading plume.

15
00:01:09.117 --> 00:01:11.137
Only the nearby sensor responds.

16
00:01:12.717 --> 00:01:17.417
Calcium beside the pore crosses the threshold; the lower level far away does not.

17
00:01:18.577 --> 00:01:23.997
The near sensor binds calcium, changes shape and exposes an orange output surface.

18
00:01:25.757 --> 00:01:30.437
Location has selected a reader without changing the calcium or the reader's binding

19
00:01:30.437 --> 00:01:31.437
properties.

20
00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:34.987
A local event can also recruit more release.

21
00:01:36.447 --> 00:01:39.987
Calcium from one site may help open a neighbouring release channel.

22
00:01:41.227 --> 00:01:42.887
Then the next site can recruit another.

23
00:01:44.107 --> 00:01:48.867
The moving front becomes a wave, and many sensors across the cell can respond.

24
00:01:50.167 --> 00:01:52.267
This spreading route is positive feedback.

25
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:55.630
Now transform distance into time.

26
00:01:56.890 --> 00:02:01.830
One sensor sees isolated calcium spikes, each followed by full recovery.

27
00:02:03.150 --> 00:02:04.970
Another sees closely spaced spikes.

28
00:02:06.170 --> 00:02:11.050
Both trains can contain similar individual rises, yet their intervals differ.

29
00:02:12.370 --> 00:02:16.570
A reader with a slowly fading active state can distinguish those intervals.

30
00:02:17.733 --> 00:02:21.833
With long gaps, the reader returns toward rest before the next spike.

31
00:02:23.373 --> 00:02:25.333
Close spikes arrive while some activity remains.

32
00:02:26.673 --> 00:02:27.953
The responses can build.

33
00:02:29.373 --> 00:02:31.433
Calcium has not acquired a new identity.

34
00:02:32.933 --> 00:02:37.733
Timing matters because the reader keeps a biochemical memory that outlasts each

35
00:02:37.733 --> 00:02:38.733
brief rise.

36
00:02:39.517 --> 00:02:44.217
Cells give calcium meaning by controlling where and when free calcium rises.

37
00:02:45.517 --> 00:02:50.217
The size of a rise matters too; this film concentrates on place and timing.

38
00:02:51.897 --> 00:02:53.577
Local domains favour nearby readers.

39
00:02:55.077 --> 00:02:57.397
Coordinated release can spread the response.

40
00:02:58.857 --> 00:03:02.717
Repeated spikes favour readers that retain activity between rises.

41
00:03:03.657 --> 00:03:09.157
Cells combine these patterns with different sensors; there is no universal decoder.

42
00:03:10.217 --> 00:03:14.337
The cell therefore writes information without changing the ion itself.

43
00:03:16.017 --> 00:03:17.837
Channel position sets the starting place.

44
00:03:19.197 --> 00:03:20.857
Feedback can enlarge the territory.

45
00:03:22.217 --> 00:03:24.037
Opening and closing set the rhythm.

46
00:03:25.437 --> 00:03:30.717
Sensors read only the calcium pattern that reaches them, on the time scale their own

47
00:03:30.717 --> 00:03:31.937
chemistry can remember.
